{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bf2f2f63c2c22c95", "type": "tool_result", "content": "<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (49.3KB). Full output saved to: /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/8f7e22c9-a797-482b-a402-959f155b7310/tool-results/bvrsv9tw7.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\nSC O PE: T RAINING -F REE AUDIO -V ISUAL E VENT\nP ERCEPTION VIA S PARSE C ROSS -M ODAL P RIOR E X CHANGE\nJaemo Jeong\n\nJunho Yoon Hyunju Kim Dongman Lee\nKAIST\n{gosfl4760, vpdtlrdl, iplay93, dlee}@kaist.ac.kr\n\narXiv:2608.07923v1 [cs.CV] 8 Aug 2026\n\nA BSTRACT\nAudio-visual event perception (AVEP) determines which events occur in a video,\nwhen they occur, and whether they are audible, visible, or both. Training-free\nmethods query new event vocabularies by matching frozen audio and visual features with text-encoded event names. However, related labels share evidence. An\nincorrect label can then score at least as high as a correct one. We call this a\nfalse co-activation (FCA). No scalar cutoff can reject the incorrect label while\nkeeping every correct one. Class-specific thresholds may prevent that label from\nbecoming a final prediction, but the FCA remains in the underlying score vector.\nWe introduce SCoPE, a training-free framework in which all queried labels compete for shared evidence and each modality guides event selection in the other.\nWe derive an exact condition for when this competition removes an FCA in a\ntwo-label fit. With identical frozen CLIP+CLAP backbones on LLP, SCoPE improves Type@seg by 7.45 points and Event@seg by 5.04 points compared with\nthe reported AV2 A values. The same fixed configuration transfers unchanged to\nOV-AVEBench and VGGSound-AVEL100k.\n\n1\n\nI NTRODUCTION\n\nAudio-visual event perception (AVEP) [9, 22, 27] determines which events occur in a video, when\nthey occur, and whether each is audible, visible, or present in both modalities. This distinction\nmatters for robotics and autonomous driving [2, 8] when a source is outside the camera view, visually\noccluded, or acoustically masked.\nMost AVEP systems learn a fixed event vocabulary. Supporting even one new class means collecting\nlabeled videos and retraining the model. Recent training-free methods [5, 20, 28] instead compare\n...\n</persisted-output>\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "8f7e22c9-a797-482b-a402-959f155b7310", "uuid": "fd85018d-1c0f-4ce1-8b82-f9898d309aac", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T01:08:10.343Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "SC O PE: T RAINING -F REE AUDIO -V ISUAL E VENT\nP ERCEPTION VIA S PARSE C ROSS -M ODAL P RIOR E X CHANGE\nJaemo Jeong\n\nJunho Yoon Hyunju Kim Dongman Lee\nKAIST\n{gosfl4760, vpdtlrdl, iplay93, dlee}@kaist.ac.kr\n\narXiv:2608.07923v1 [cs.CV] 8 Aug 2026\n\nA BSTRACT\nAudio-visual event perception (AVEP) determines which events occur in a video,\nwhen they occur, and whether they are audible, visible, or both. Training-free\nmethods query new event vocabularies by matching frozen audio and visual features with text-encoded event names. However, related labels share evidence. An\nincorrect label can then score at least as high as a correct one. We call this a\nfalse co-activation (FCA). No scalar cutoff can reject the incorrect label while\nkeeping every correct one. Class-specific thresholds may prevent that label from\nbecoming a final prediction, but the FCA remains in the underlying score vector.\nWe introduce SCoPE, a training-free framework in which all queried labels compete for shared evidence and each modality guides event selection in the other.\nWe derive an exact condition for when this competition removes an FCA in a\ntwo-label fit. With identical frozen CLIP+CLAP backbones on LLP, SCoPE improves Type@seg by 7.45 points and Event@seg by 5.04 points compared with\nthe reported AV2 A values. The same fixed configuration transfers unchanged to\nOV-AVEBench and VGGSound-AVEL100k.\n\n1\n\nI NTRODUCTION\n\nAudio-visual event perception (AVEP) [9, 22, 27] determines which events occur in a video, when\nthey occur, and whether each is audible, visible, or present in both modalities. This distinction\nmatters for robotics and autonomous driving [2, 8] when a source is outside the camera view, visually\noccluded, or acoustically masked.\nMost AVEP systems learn a fixed event vocabulary. Supporting even one new class means collecting\nlabeled videos and retraining the model. Recent training-free methods [5, 20, 28] instead compare\nfrozen audio and visual features with text-encoded event names. Each event name is matched to\nevery time interval separately, and the resulting decisions are adjusted through fixed or adaptive\nscore thresholds.\nHowever, related event names lie close in the embedding space, so the same evidence can raise\nseveral labels. An incorrect label can then score at least as high as a correct one. We call this a\nfalse co-activation (FCA). No scalar cutoff can reject the incorrect label while keeping every correct\none. Class-specific thresholds may prevent that label from becoming a final prediction, but the FCA\nremains in the independently matched scores. Section 2 gives the exact definition.\nWe divide FCAs into two types. The first occurs when an incorrect label is sandwiched between a\nstronger and a weaker correct label in the score ranking. In Figure 1(a), the video contains Dog and\nCar, but not Cat. Yet Cat responds to the visual evidence for Dog and ranks above Car. Its score is\nlargely borrowed from the stronger correct label, so we call this case a shadow. The second occurs\nwhen the available evidence does not clearly distinguish the incorrect label from a correct one. In\nthe audio branch, a car engine can also sound like a motorcycle, so Motorcycle remains plausible\nbeside Car. We call this case a rival. Section 2 defines both types exactly.\n1\n\n\f(a) Two false co-activation types\n\n(c) False Co-Activation per Segment\n\n(b) Our sparsity and prior exchange\n\nshadow\n\nrival\n\nDog\n\nDog\n\nCat\n\nCat\nvisual, Stage 1\n\nCar\n\nFCA / Segment\n8\n\nCar\n\n7.2\n\nvisual\n\n0\n\nτ\n\n0\n\n1\n\nτ\n\n1\n\nsparse coefficient w\n\ndense score s\n6\n\nMotorcycle\nMotorcycle\n\nCar\naudio, Stage 1\n\nCar\n\nDog\n\n4\n\n3.4\n\naudio\n\n0\n\nDog\n\nτ\n\n1\n\n2.9\n\nsparse coefficient w\nτ\n\n0\n\n1\n\n2.9\n\n2\n\ndense score s\n\n1.1\n\nMotorcycle\nevidence of the present label\n\nambiguous within the modality\n\n0.2\n\nCar\naudio, Stage 2\n\n0\nDense\n\nDog\n\nAV²A\n\nvisual\n0\n\nτ\n\nSCoPE\n\nDense\n\nAV²A\n\nSCoPE\n\naudio\n\n1\n\nsparse coefficient w\n\nFigure 1: False co-activation and SCoPE. (a) No scalar cutoff keeps both annotated events while\nrejecting the extra candidate between them. In a two-label sparse fit, Dog remains while Cat drops\nout. Motorcycle remains beside Car. (b) Stage 1 selects sparse support, and Stage 2 uses cross-modal\nsupport to change the target’s per-class ℓ1 selection costs before re-solving. (c) Mean FCA counts on\nmulti-label LLP segments, split by the two pairwise diagnostic tags.\n\nWe propose SCoPE, a training-free framework for Sparse Cross-modal Prior Exchange. Stage 1\nmakes all labels compete for the evidence in each time interval, reducing FCAs before cross-modal\nguidance. Stage 2 uses events selected in one modality to lower the cost of selecting the same\nevents in the other. The target modality then makes its own decision from its own features rather\nthan copying the source score or prediction. A standard sparse readout cuts the sorted positive\ncoefficients at their largest drop and therefore needs no calibrated cutoff. SCoPE requires no taskspecific training, instruction tuning, or LLM decoding.\nOur contributions are:\n• We define score-level false co-activation and show why one scalar cutoff on a fixed score\nvector cannot reject an incorrect label without losing a correct one. We derive an exact\ncondition for the two-label fit and test its prediction with the full 25-label dictionary.\n• We introduce sparse cross-modal prior exchange. Events selected in one modality lower the\ncost of selecting the same events in the other. Each modality still makes its own prediction\ninstead of copying source scores or labels.\n• Compared with the reported AV2 A values obtained with frozen CLIP+CLAP encoders,\nSCoPE improves Type@seg by 7.45 points and Event@seg by 5.04 points on LLP.\n• SCoPE’s sparse inference precedes final decision rules, so it can be combined with existing training-free post-processing. Adding AV2 A-style dynamic thresholds and candidate\nfiltering raises Type@seg from 51.25 to 53.61 and Event@seg from 39.74 to 47.62 without\nretraining. Inference takes 3.16 ms per video after feature encoding.\n\n2\n\nFALSE C O -ACTIVATION\n\nLet a video be divided into T aligned audio and visual segments, and let Y be the queried event\nvocabulary. For modality m ∈ {a, v}, Ym (t) ⊆ Y is the ground-truth event set in segment t.\nA frozen modality encoder produces zm (t), and the corresponding frozen text encoder maps the\ncandidate names to\nCm = [c1m , . . . , c|Y|\nm ].\nThe dictionary is encoded once and reused across videos. Dense training-free inference scores each\ncandidate independently as\nc\nscos\nm (t, c) = cos(zm (t), cm ).\n\nLet qm (t, c) denote the value that a method assigns to candidate c before making its final prediction.\nFor example, after candidate filtering, qm (t, c) is the filtered score of each remaining candidate.\n2\n\n\fDefinition 1 (False Co-Activation). Suppose a method retains at least one ground-truth label in a\nsegment. A retained label j that is not in the ground truth is a false co-activation when\nj∈\n/ Ym (t),\n\nqm (t, j) ≥ min qm (t, i).\ni∈Ym (t)\ni retained\n\n(1)\n\nCandidate j is at or above the lowest-valued ground-truth label that remains. A scalar cutoff on the\nsame values therefore cannot remove j while keeping every remaining ground-truth label. Definition 1 includes the more severe case in which j outranks every remaining ground-truth label.\nFCA is a score-level diagnostic rather than a synonym for a final false positive. A class-specific\ncutoff can keep the candidate out of the final prediction, but the FCA remains in the pre-readout\nvalues.\nIn a joint sparse fit, correlated labels no longer receive shared evidence separately. Once one label\nexplains their common direction, another is selected only if its remaining contribution justifies an\nadditional selection cost. We next analyze the smallest sparse problem that contains this competition,\nwith one annotated event and one competing candidate.\nLemma 1 (Two-Label Sparse Competition). Consider a centered unit embedding z (centering is\ndefined in Section 3.2), a text atom ci for an annotated event, and a text atom cj for a candidate\noutside the annotated set. Their two-label non-negative sparse fit is\n2\n\nw∗ = arg min ∥z − [ci , cj ]w∥2 + λ0 ∥w∥1 .\nw≥0\n\n(2)\n\n⊤\n⊤\nLet ρ = c⊤\ni cj with 0 ≤ ρ < 1, and order the atoms so that ci z ≥ cj z. We restrict the analysis to\nnon-negative correlation because the shadow/rival diagnostic concerns labels that share a positively\naligned text direction. Negatively correlated atom pairs fall outside this diagnostic. Define γ = λ0 /2\nand assume c⊤\ni z > γ. The solution selects both atoms exactly when\n\n∆j|i (z) := (cj − ρci )⊤ z − γ(1 − ρ) > 0.\n\n(3)\n\nIf ∆j|i (z) ≤ 0, the fit selects only ci and removes candidate cj . Appendix A.1 gives the proof.\nDefinition 2 (Shadow and Rival Co-Activations). Take an FCA j. We compare it with each\nrelated annotated event i that has at least as much dense evidence and can enter the sparse fit. These\nreference events form\n\b\n⊤\n⊤\n⊤\nIj (z) = i ∈ Ym (t) : c⊤\ni z ≥ cj z, ci z > γ, 0 < ci cj < 1 .\nThe lemma permits ρ = 0, but the diagnostic below requires ρ > 0 because a shadow specifically\ndenotes evidence shared by related atoms. For each i ∈ Ij (z), consider the two-label sparse problem\nin Equation (2). We define\nj is a shadow\n\n⇐⇒\n\n∃i ∈ Ij (z) : ∆j|i (z) ≤ 0,\n\nand call it a rival otherwise. In Figure 1(a), Dog remains selected while Cat disappears, so Cat is a\nshadow. A rival is any FCA for which no eligible stronger annotated event removes the candidate\nin this two-label analysis. This includes Ij (z) = ∅, where no such reference event exists. We\nreport this split only on multi-label segments to study a false candidate competing with several\nsimultaneously annotated events rather than an ordinary single-label ranking error.\nLemma 1 is exact only for two labels. With a full dictionary, several labels can jointly explain the\nresidual and remove a rival FCA. Shadow and rival are therefore diagnostic tags, not hidden groundtruth causes or a one-to-one assignment to Stages 1 and 2. Appendix A.1 tests how the two-label\ncondition relates to the full 25-label solve.\n\n3\n\nM ETHOD\n\n3.1\n\nP ROBLEM F ORMULATION\n\nGiven T aligned audio and visual segments and a queried event vocabulary Y, training-free AVEP\npredicts three binary decisions ŷa (t, c), ŷv (t, c), and ŷav (t, c) for every segment t and candidate c.\nThe first two indicate event presence in the audio and visual branches, while ŷav denotes an event\n3\n\n\fStage 0 · Dense Cosine\n\nStage 1 · Sparse Selection\n\nStage 2 · Re-Selection\n\nprediction heads\nvisual\nencoder\n\n❄\n\nz̃_v\n\nDog\n\nCat\n\nCar\n\nMotorcycle\n\nDog\n\nCat\n\nCar\n\nMotorcycle\n\nDog\n\nCat\n\nCar\n\nMotorcycle\n\n1\n\n1\n\n1\n\n1\n\n1.25↑\n\n1.25↑\n\n0.8↓\n\n0.7↓\n\nS_V = {Dog, Car}\n\nprior v→a\nS_AV = {Car}\n\nprior a→v\n\naudio\nencoder\n\n❄\n\nz̃_a\n\nDog\n\nCat\n\nCar\n\nMotorcycle\n\ncoefficient w per label\n\n1\n\n1\n\n1\n\n1\n\n1\n\n0.7↓\n\n1.35↑\n\n0.6↓\n\n1.35↑\n\nDog\n\nCat\n\nCar\n\nMotorcycle\n\nDog\n\nCat\n\nCar\n\nMotorcycle\n\nselection cost λ_c\n\n0.7↓\n\n0.7↓\n\nS_A = {Car}\n\nlowered by the prior from the other branch (audio → visual, visual → audio)\n\nFigure 2: SCoPE on the example from Figure 1. Stage 1 selects sparse support in each branch.\nStage 2 lowers the selection costs of source-supported labels and re-solves from the target embedding. The AV head fuses coefficients retained by both branches.\nthat is jointly audible and visible. The method receives the frozen segment embeddings zm (t) and\ntext dictionaries Cm defined in Section 2, and uses no task-specific parameter training or segment\nlabels.\nSCoPE has two stages. Stage 1 fits the full event dictionary separately in each modality, making\nthe candidates compete for the segment evidence. Stage 2 uses support from the other modality\nto change candidate costs and then re-solves from the target embedding. Figure 2 gives the full\npipeline.\n3.2\n\nC ENTERED E VENT D ICTIONARY\n\nMean subtraction [18] is a standard first step for removing common components from embedding\nspaces. Contrastive multimodal encoders [14] also occupy modality-dependent offset regions. We\ntherefore center and renormalize each modality before non-negative sparse inference:\nz̃m (t) =\n\nzm (t) − µm\n,\n∥zm (t) − µm ∥2\n\nc̃cm =\n\nccm − µ̄m\n.\n∥ccm − µ̄m ∥2\n\n(4)\n\nHere µm is estimated from an external reference set and µ̄m is the mean of the queried text atoms.\nWe write C̃m for the centered dictionary. Appendix B.1 specifies the references and Appendix C.2\ntests sensitivity to the reference datasets.\n3.3\n\nS TAGE 1: C OMPETITIVE S PARSE S ELECTION\n\nStage 1 makes all candidate labels compete to explain the same centered segment embedding. It\nsolves non-negative Lasso independently in each modality:\n2\n\nwm (t) = arg min z̃m (t) − C̃m w\nw≥0\n\n2\n\n+ λ0 ∥w∥1 .\n\n(5)\n\nEquation (5) differs from dense matching in how evidence is assigned. Cosine matching compares\nevery text atom with the original embedding. The joint fit instead keeps an additional atom only\nwhen it reduces the remaining reconstruction error enough to justify another nonzero coefficient.\nCorrelated labels therefore compete for shared evidence. This is the effect studied by the two-label\ndiagnostic, but the diagnostic does not determine the outcome of the full Stage 1 solve.\nThe selected support is\nSm (t) = {c : wm (t, c) > εsupp }.\nEquation (5) extends the two-atom fit in Equation (2) to all queried labels. Only this sparse,\ncoefficient-weighted support is passed across modalities.\n4\n\n\f3.4\n\nS TAGE 2: C ROSS -M ODAL C OST P RIOR AND TARGET R E -S ELECTION\n\nStage 2 uses support from the other modality to reconsider candidates that Stage 1 alone cannot\nresolve. It exchanges a pooled soft prior, not dense scores or binary predictions. We describe visualto-audio guidance. The reverse direction uses the same construction with its own strength ηa→v .\nFigure 1(b) illustrates the distinction. Visual support can lower the audio selection costs of Dog and\nCar, but it does not add either visual score to the audio coefficients. Audio Stage 2 re-solves from\nits own embedding, so Car can gain support without forcing the visually observed but silent Dog\ninto the output.\nReconstruction-weighted source prior. We measure how well the Stage 1 reconstruction fits each\nsource segment and pool its normalized coefficients:\nT\nh\ni\n1X\nwv (t, c)\nrv (t) = cos(z̃v (t), C̃v wv (t)) ,\nPv (c) =\nrv (t)\n.\n(6)\nT t=1\n∥wv (t)∥∞ + εnorm\n+\nHere, [x]+ = max(0, x) and εnorm is a numerical stabilizer. We set rv (t) = 0 when wv (t) has\nempty support. The prior retains class identity while allowing evidence at one time to guide another.\nThe factor rv (t) givesP\nless weight to poorly reconstructed source segments. We divide by the video\nlength rather than by t rv (t), so consistently poor reconstruction also reduces the total guidance.\nIf every rv (t) is zero, Stage 2 uses the Stage 1 cost. The infinity-norm normalization sets the largest\ncoefficient in each nonempty segment to one, making source strength comparable across time while\npreserving the relative coefficient sizes within that segment.\nFixed-budget event selection costs. The source prior first defines an unnormalized audio cost\nmultiplier\naa,c\n.\n(7)\naa,c = exp(−ηv→a Pv (c)) ,\nλa,c = λ0 1 P\naa,j\nP |Y| j\nThe normalization keeps every cost positive and enforces c λa,c = |Y|λ0 . The prior therefore\nredistributes a fixed budget rather than weakening sparsity as a whole. Stronger source support\nlowers a class’s relative cost, while the other costs rise to preserve the mean. These costs are shared\nacross the target video, but every segment is re-solved from its own embedding.\nTarget re-selection. The audio branch then re-solves its own weighted NN-Lasso problem:\nX\n2\nλa,c |wc |.\nwanew (t) = arg min z̃a (t) − C̃a w +\nw≥0\n\n2\n\n(8)\n\nc\n\nThe reverse direction produces wvnew (t). A lower cost can help the target reconsider a weak label,\nbut it cannot insert the source label or guarantee a correct selection. The unchanged reconstruction\nterm still determines whether the target retains the candidate.\n3.5\n\nS PARSE R EADOUT\n\nThe sparse coefficients can vary in absolute scale across segments, so a single calibrated cutoff is\nnot appropriate for every output [15]. We instead use a classical one-dimensional gap split [11],\nequivalent to cutting the longest edge of single-linkage clustering on the sorted coefficient values.\nnew\nnew\nLet the positive Stage 2 coefficients be sorted as wm,(1)\n(t) ≥ · · · ≥ wm,(K)\n(t) > 0 and append the\nnew\nzero anchor wm,(K+1) (t) = 0. We select the largest gap:\n\u0012\nh\ni\u0013\n∗\nnew\nnew\n∗ (t)) }.\nkm (t) = min arg max wm,(k) (t) − wm,(k+1) (t) ,\nŶm (t) = {c(1) , . . . , c(km\n(9)\n1≤k≤K\n\nWe set ŷm (t, c) = I[c ∈ Ŷm (t)]. The outer minimum retains the smaller support when gaps tie.\nEmpty support produces no prediction, and singleton support retains its label. The zero anchor lets\nthe rule keep the full support when the final drop is strictly largest. The readout has no calibration\nthreshold or support-size parameter.\nnew\nnew\nFor audio-visual prediction, define Sm\n(t) = {c : wm\n(t, c) > εsupp }. We retain the support\nintersection and fuse only its coefficients:\nSav (t) = Sanew (t) ∩ Svnew (t),\nwav (t, c) = I[c ∈ Sav (t)] [αwanew (t, c) + (1 − α)wvnew (t, c)] .\n(10)\n\n5\n\n\fApplying the same sparse readout to wav gives ŷav . For comparability, we inherit AV2 A’s\nCLIP+CLAP fusion weight [20] and do not retune it by dataset. The audio and visual outputs\nremain unchanged by this AV fusion. Appendix B.1 gives solver and temporal decoding details.\n\n4\n\nE XPERIMENTS\n\n4.1\n\nS ETUP\n\nDatasets and metrics. LLP [22] contains 25 event classes and modality-wise segment annotations\nfor audio, visual, and audio-visual events. We report the standard segment-level and event-level F1\nscores for Audio, Visual, Audio-Visual, Type, and Event. OV-AVEBench [28] contains 67 queried\nclasses, for which we report chunk accuracy, segment F1, event F1, and their average. VGGSoundAVEL100k uses 141 VGGSound [3] event classes and evaluates temporal event localization using\nsegment micro F1 and event F1. Appendix B.2 gives the split, temporal annotation, evaluated sample, and metric details.\nFrozen encoders and dictionaries. The primary experiments use CLIP ViT-B/32 for vision and\nLAION-CLAP HTSAT-tiny for audio. For CLIP+CLAP, we follow AV2 A’s released implementation. CLAP uses This is a sound of {label}, and CLIP uses A {label}. LanguageBind uses A {label} in both modalities. ImageBind uses the same modality-specific templates\nas CLIP+CLAP. LLP uses 25 atoms, OV-AVEBench evaluates 67 event classes, and VGGSoundAVEL100k uses 141 atoms. Each dictionary is encoded once and reused for every video. Within\neach table block, compared methods use the same frozen encoders, prompt templates, and vocabulary. We evaluate SCoPE with both CLIP+CLAP and LanguageBind on LLP, and with ImageBind\non OV-AVEBench and VGGSound-AVEL100k. The CLIP+CLAP OV-AVEBench block uses exactly the 67 evaluated event prompts. The ImageBind base and SCoPE rows share those prompts\nplus an auxiliary other prompt, which is treated as background during evaluation.\nReferences and implementation. We compute external feature means from MS-COCO 2017 for\nvision and ESC-50 for audio. Section 3 specifies the inference settings. The base rows in Table 2 use\nthe same frozen dense decoder across datasets. The ImageBind agreement row applies OV-AVEL’s\nA/V agreement rule to the same dense scores. We quote the published LLP Dense and AV2 A rows\nand reproduce the OV-AVEBench AV2 A row from its released predictions with the official evaluator.\nAppendix B.1 gives the evaluation populations, solver, and preprocessing details.\nHyperparameters. On the 563 official LLP validation videos with complete frozen feature caches,\nwe select the sparse cost and directional prior strengths. This gives λ0 = 0.30, ηv→a = 16, and\nηa→v = 4. We use support tolerance εsupp = 10−6 and normalization stabilizer εnorm = 10−8 .\nThe AV fusion weight α = 0.45 is inherited from AV2 A rather than selected on this subset. We\nfreeze these values for LLP test evaluation and transfer them unchanged to the other backbones and\ndatasets. Appendix B.2 reports the search grid, while Appendix C.1 reports sensitivity to the fixed\nsettings.\nRuntime protocol. We report cached-feature inference separately from the 393 ms encoding reference used by OV-AVEL. The AV2 A latency is the single-GPU equivalent of its reported 4×TITAN\nRTX run. Appendix B.1 gives the measured scope and hardware.\n4.2\n\nM AIN R ESULTS\n\nLLP. Table 1 compares training-free methods using the same frozen backbones and vocabularies\nwithin each block. With CLIP+CLAP, Stage 2 raises Audio, Visual, and Audio-Visual segment F1\nover Stage 1 by 7.90, 8.15, and 11.70 points. Compared with the reported AV2 A values, it raises\nType@seg from 43.8 to 51.25 and Event@seg from 34.7 to 39.74. With LanguageBind, the same\nfixed configuration reaches 56.31 Type@seg and 46.01 Event@seg.\nBenchmark and backbone transfer. Table 2 evaluates the same frozen SCoPE configuration on\nlarger vocabularies and alternative backbones. On OV-AVEBench, SCoPE reaches 50.66 Avg. with\nCLIP+CLAP and 53.90 with ImageBind, including 51.21 and 55.69 on unseen classes. On strict\nVGGSound-AVEL100k, SCoPE reaches 49.63 segment F1 and 30.88 event F1 with CLIP+CLAP,\ncompared with 28.47 and 24.21 for the AV2 A baseline. With ImageBind, OV-AVEL-style agreement\n6\n\n\fTable 1: Training-free audio-visual video parsing on LLP (F1, %). LanguageBind follows AV2 A’s\nadditional-backbone evaluation.\nAudio\n\nMethod\n\nVisual\n\nAudio-Visual\n\nType\n\nEvent\n\nSeg\n\nEvt\n\nSeg\n\nEvt\n\nSeg\n\nEvt\n\nSeg\n\nEvt\n\nSeg\n\nEvt\n\nCLIP+CLAP [6, 19]\nwith AV2 A\nwith SCoPE (Stage 1)\nwith SCoPE (Stage 2)\n\n17.6\n32.7\n32.42\n40.32\n\n15.1\n28.5\n23.98\n31.47\n\n18.8\n43.5\n50.91\n59.06\n\n17.8\n41.5\n42.29\n52.39\n\n30.0\n55.1\n42.67\n54.37\n\n25.6\n48.2\n36.41\n48.05\n\n22.1\n43.8\n42.00\n51.25\n\n19.5\n39.4\n34.23\n43.97\n\n18.3\n34.7\n31.61\n39.74\n\n16.2\n31.2\n23.17\n31.04\n\nLanguageBind [29]\nwith AV2 A\nwith SCoPE (Stage 1)\nwith SCoPE (Stage 2)\n\n20.3\n40.9\n31.45\n45.81\n\n17.5\n35.9\n18.90\n37.21\n\n21.3\n57.4\n62.38\n64.61\n\n20.0\n54.4\n55.87\n59.47\n\n24.3\n59.1\n49.08\n58.52\n\n21.0\n52.3\n41.84\n52.31\n\n22.0\n52.4\n47.64\n56.31\n\n19.5\n47.5\n38.87\n49.66\n\n20.8\n43.4\n36.48\n46.01\n\n18.4\n38.9\n24.98\n37.35\n\nTable 2: Training-free event localization across datasets and frozen backbones (%).\nOV-AVEBench\nMethod\n\nVGGSound-AVEL100k\n\nAcc. Seg-F1 Event-F1 Avg. Unseen Avg. Seg F1\n\nCLIP+CLAP [6, 19]\n44.46\nwith OV-AVEL [28] 51.86\nwith AV2 A\n43.18\nwith SCoPE\n57.11\n\nEvent F1\n\n37.57\n42.92\n31.90\n49.81\n\n30.89\n32.85\n24.34\n45.06\n\n37.64\n42.54\n33.14\n50.66\n\n38.17\n42.05\n32.69\n51.21\n\n19.09\n26.98\n28.47\n49.63\n\n6.09\n14.83\n24.21\n30.88\n\nImageBind [10]\n36.98 30.61\nwith OV-AVEL [28] 59.41 48.07\nwith SCoPE\n60.58 53.01\n\n24.53\n35.66\n48.11\n\n30.71\n47.71\n53.90\n\n30.53\n48.14\n55.69\n\n15.46\n45.02\n56.92\n\n5.43\n22.97\n38.69\n\nraises the dense baseline from 15.46 to 45.02 segment F1, and SCoPE further reaches 56.92. Event\nF1 rises from 5.43 to 22.97 and 38.69, respectively.\n4.3\n\nM ECHANISM A NALYSIS\n\nTables 3 and 4 summarize the controlled exchange variants and inference cost, respectively.\n\nVariant\n\nSoft\nprior\n\nReconstruction\nweighting\n\nTarget\nre-selection\n\nType@seg\n\nEvent@seg\n\nNo exchange\nHard prior\nDirect fusion\nSoft prior, unweighted\nSCoPE\n\n×\n×\n✓\n✓\n✓\n\n×\n✓\n✓\n×\n✓\n\n×\n✓\n×\n✓\n✓\n\n42.00\n50.35\n44.64\n50.99\n51.25\n\n31.61\n39.21\n32.04\n39.68\n39.74\n\nTable 3: Cross-modal prior ablation on LLP (F1, %).\nOV-AVEBench runtime (ms/video)\nMethod\nOV-AVEL\nAV2 A\nSCoPE\n\nEncoding Inference\n393\n393\n393\n\nNN-Lasso scaling (ms/segment)\nLabels K\nLatency\n\nTotal\n\n4.80 397.80\n1174\n1567\n3.16 396.16\n\n61\n1k\n5k 10k\n0.17 0.18 0.34 0.65\n\nTable 4: Runtime and vocabulary scaling.\n\nWhat should be exchanged, and how? All rows start from identical Stage 1 coefficients and vary\nthree choices: prior form, reconstruction weighting, and target re-selection. A hard prior gives\nevery selected source label the same value, whereas a soft prior gives stronger selected labels more\ninfluence. Direct fusion adds the soft, reconstruction-weighted prior to the target coefficients without\na new solve. Its directional ratio is fixed to SCoPE’s 4:1 setting, and only one global scale is selected\non validation. The unweighted soft-prior row keeps target re-selection but lets selected segments\n7\n\n\fcontribute equally. SCoPE uses the soft prior, reconstruction weighting, and target re-selection\ntogether.\nFor the unweighted row, we rescale η using validation features without labels so its mean absolute\nselection-cost change matches SCoPE. This isolates reconstruction weighting from overall prior\nstrength.\nSCoPE gives the strongest result on both metrics. Target re-selection provides the clearest controlled\ngain. Direct fusion trails SCoPE by 6.61 Type@seg points and 7.70 Event@seg points. The soft\nprior adds 0.90 Type@seg points over the hard prior, and reconstruction weighting adds 0.26 over\nthe matched unweighted prior. Appendix C.5 provides clip-level examples of target re-selection.\nBoundary of the Audio-Visual readout. AV2 A’s score\nshadow\nrival\n2.08\nfusion can activate an Audio-Visual event from one strong\n1.57\nbranch, whereas SCoPE requires support in both Stage 2\n2\nbranches. Replacing AV A’s fusion with the strict intersection of its branch predictions lowers Audio-Visual seg0.24\nment F1 from 55.1 to 51.8. SCoPE reaches 54.37 while reAV A\nDense\nSCoPE\ntaining its both-branch support rule, recovering 2.57 points\nover strict intersection. Among the predictions added by Figure 3: Mean AV false co-activations per\nAV2 A’s fusion beyond strict intersection, 58.5% are incor- multi-label LLP segment. Each label\ninherits its branch-level shadow or rival\nrect.\n2\n\ntag.\n\nFigure 3 aggregates the same boundary over multi-label\nAV segments.1 Score fusion retains more one-sided labels, whereas SCoPE requires support from\nboth modalities.\n4.4\n\nE FFICIENCY\n\nSCoPE solves NN-Lasso only at inference time over a text dictionary encoded once. Over all 5,820\nOV-AVEBench videos, the cached-feature pipeline takes 3.16 ms per video, or 316 videos/s. Table 4\nadds this measurement to the shared encoding reference. The 396.16 ms total is therefore an arithmetic composite rather than a separately timed end-to-end run. We also measure visual NN-Lasso\nlatency at 61, 1k, 5k, and 10k labels using cached LLP features and 200 FISTA iterations. With\nbatches of 128 segments, latency stays below 0.35 ms per segment through 5k labels and reaches\n0.65 ms with 10k labels. At batch size 1, the 10k-label solve takes 0.022 s per segment.\n\n5\n\nR ELATED W ORK\n\n5.1\n\nF IXED -VOCABULARY AVEP\n\nAudio-visual event localization [21] identifies events that are both audible and visible in temporal\nvideo segments. Audio-visual video parsing [22] broadens this task to audio-only, visual-only, and\njointly audio-visual events under video-level supervision. Later methods improve temporal localization and modality assignment with task-specific models [7, 9, 12, 23–25]. Because these models are\ntrained for a fixed label set, extending them to new event names or domains requires new annotations\nor optimization.\n5.2\n\nO PEN -VOCABULARY AVEP\n\nOpen-vocabulary AVEP queries events through text rather than a fixed classifier. OV-AVEL [28]\nintroduced OV-AVEBench and a CLIP+CLAP baseline that applies fixed thresholds to frozen textmatching scores. AV2 A [20] adds video-level candidate filtering and adaptive class thresholds, and\nsubsequent training-free work [5] further adapts score thresholds. These methods refine decisions\nover frozen text-matching scores. SCoPE intervenes at a different point by first forming sparse,\ncompetitive supports and then exchanging them across modalities.\n1\nDense methods do not define a separate AV score, so we fuse their branch cosine scores with the AV\nreadout weight α. An AV FCA is tagged as a shadow if either modality branch gives it that tag, and otherwise\nas a rival.\n\n8\n\n\f5.3\n\nS PARSE E VENT I NFERENCE\n\nSparse coding [13, 16] explains an observation with a compact set of dictionary atoms that compete for shared evidence. Recent work [1, 26] applies this idea to foundation-model embeddings\nby decomposing CLIP features over text-defined concepts and constructing sparse concept representations for audio features. These methods analyze each modality independently. SCoPE instead\nindexes the audio and visual dictionaries with the same event vocabulary. This shared label space\nlets source support redistribute a fixed selection-cost budget in the target, which then solves its own\nsparse problem again. Sparse selection therefore provides both within-modality competition and\ncross-modal event guidance.\n\n6\n\nD ISCUSSION AND C ONCLUSION\n\nFuture directions. SCoPE uses a video-level prior and offline temporal post-processing. Extending\nboth to causal inference is a natural next step. Adaptive costs and stronger branch evidence from\nsource separation or object localization are also promising directions that preserve training-free\ninference.\nLimitations. Lemma 1 is exact only for two atoms and does not completely characterize fulldictionary selection. Stage 2 requires useful source support and target evidence, but does not know\nwhether the source is correct. Encoder confusion or missing target evidence can therefore leave false\nactivations or missed events. The sparse readout can remove weak events when event strengths differ\nsubstantially. Directional prior strengths and the conservative AV intersection are fixed operating\npoints that may vary by deployment.\nConclusion. SCoPE replaces independent label scoring with sparse competition and targetcontrolled cross-modal re-selection. Stage 1 suppresses related candidates by making them compete\nfor the same evidence. Stage 2 pools reconstruction-weighted source support, but the target reconstruction still decides whether a candidate is selected. The AV head ma", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false, "persistedOutputPath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/8f7e22c9-a797-482b-a402-959f155b7310/tool-results/bvrsv9tw7.txt", "persistedOutputSize": 50501}}
