{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bf9672c47e30db56", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tSC O PE: T RAINING -F REE AUDIO -V ISUAL E VENT\n2\tP ERCEPTION VIA S PARSE C ROSS -M ODAL P RIOR E X CHANGE\n3\tJaemo Jeong\n4\t\n5\tJunho Yoon Hyunju Kim Dongman Lee\n6\tKAIST\n7\t{gosfl4760, vpdtlrdl, iplay93, dlee}@kaist.ac.kr\n8\t\n9\tarXiv:2608.07923v1 [cs.CV] 8 Aug 2026\n10\t\n11\tA BSTRACT\n12\tAudio-visual event perception (AVEP) determines which events occur in a video,\n13\twhen they occur, and whether they are audible, visible, or both. Training-free\n14\tmethods query new event vocabularies by matching frozen audio and visual features with text-encoded event names. However, related labels share evidence. An\n15\tincorrect label can then score at least as high as a correct one. We call this a\n16\tfalse co-activation (FCA). No scalar cutoff can reject the incorrect label while\n17\tkeeping every correct one. Class-specific thresholds may prevent that label from\n18\tbecoming a final prediction, but the FCA remains in the underlying score vector.\n19\tWe introduce SCoPE, a training-free framework in which all queried labels compete for shared evidence and each modality guides event selection in the other.\n20\tWe derive an exact condition for when this competition removes an FCA in a\n21\ttwo-label fit. With identical frozen CLIP+CLAP backbones on LLP, SCoPE improves Type@seg by 7.45 points and Event@seg by 5.04 points compared with\n22\tthe reported AV2 A values. The same fixed configuration transfers unchanged to\n23\tOV-AVEBench and VGGSound-AVEL100k.\n24\t\n25\t1\n26\t\n27\tI NTRODUCTION\n28\t\n29\tAudio-visual event perception (AVEP) [9, 22, 27] determines which events occur in a video, when\n30\tthey occur, and whether each is audible, visible, or present in both modalities. This distinction\n31\tmatters for robotics and autonomous driving [2, 8] when a source is outside the camera view, visually\n32\toccluded, or acoustically masked.\n33\tMost AVEP systems learn a fixed event vocabulary. Supporting even one new class means collecting\n34\tlabeled videos and retraining the model. Recent training-free methods [5, 20, 28] instead compare\n35\tfrozen audio and visual features with text-encoded event names. Each event name is matched to\n36\tevery time interval separately, and the resulting decisions are adjusted through fixed or adaptive\n37\tscore thresholds.\n38\tHowever, related event names lie close in the embedding space, so the same evidence can raise\n39\tseveral labels. An incorrect label can then score at least as high as a correct one. We call this a\n40\tfalse co-activation (FCA). No scalar cutoff can reject the incorrect label while keeping every correct\n41\tone. Class-specific thresholds may prevent that label from becoming a final prediction, but the FCA\n42\tremains in the independently matched scores. Section 2 gives the exact definition.\n43\tWe divide FCAs into two types. The first occurs when an incorrect label is sandwiched between a\n44\tstronger and a weaker correct label in the score ranking. In Figure 1(a), the video contains Dog and\n45\tCar, but not Cat. Yet Cat responds to the visual evidence for Dog and ranks above Car. Its score is\n46\tlargely borrowed from the stronger correct label, so we call this case a shadow. The second occurs\n47\twhen the available evidence does not clearly distinguish the incorrect label from a correct one. In\n48\tthe audio branch, a car engine can also sound like a motorcycle, so Motorcycle remains plausible\n49\tbeside Car. We call this case a rival. Section 2 defines both types exactly.\n50\t1\n51\t\n52\t\f(a) Two false co-activation types\n53\t\n54\t(c) False Co-Activation per Segment\n55\t\n56\t(b) Our sparsity and prior exchange\n57\t\n58\tshadow\n59\t\n60\trival\n61\t\n62\tDog\n63\t\n64\tDog\n65\t\n66\tCat\n67\t\n68\tCat\n69\tvisual, Stage 1\n70\t\n71\tCar\n72\t\n73\tFCA / Segment\n74\t8\n75\t\n76\tCar\n77\t\n78\t7.2\n79\t\n80\tvisual\n81\t\n82\t0\n83\t\n84\tτ\n85\t\n86\t0\n87\t\n88\t1\n89\t\n90\tτ\n91\t\n92\t1\n93\t\n94\tsparse coefficient w\n95\t\n96\tdense score s\n97\t6\n98\t\n99\tMotorcycle\n100\tMotorcycle\n101\t\n102\tCar\n103\taudio, Stage 1\n104\t\n105\tCar\n106\t\n107\tDog\n108\t\n109\t4\n110\t\n111\t3.4\n112\t\n113\taudio\n114\t\n115\t0\n116\t\n117\tDog\n118\t\n119\tτ\n120\t\n121\t1\n122\t\n123\t2.9\n124\t\n125\tsparse coefficient w\n126\tτ\n127\t\n128\t0\n129\t\n130\t1\n131\t\n132\t2.9\n133\t\n134\t2\n135\t\n136\tdense score s\n137\t\n138\t1.1\n139\t\n140\tMotorcycle\n141\tevidence of the present label\n142\t\n143\tambiguous within the modality\n144\t\n145\t0.2\n146\t\n147\tCar\n148\taudio, Stage 2\n149\t\n150\t0\n151\tDense\n152\t\n153\tDog\n154\t\n155\tAV²A\n156\t\n157\tvisual\n158\t0\n159\t\n160\tτ\n161\t\n162\tSCoPE\n163\t\n164\tDense\n165\t\n166\tAV²A\n167\t\n168\tSCoPE\n169\t\n170\taudio\n171\t\n172\t1\n173\t\n174\tsparse coefficient w\n175\t\n176\tFigure 1: False co-activation and SCoPE. (a) No scalar cutoff keeps both annotated events while\n177\trejecting the extra candidate between them. In a two-label sparse fit, Dog remains while Cat drops\n178\tout. Motorcycle remains beside Car. (b) Stage 1 selects sparse support, and Stage 2 uses cross-modal\n179\tsupport to change the target’s per-class ℓ1 selection costs before re-solving. (c) Mean FCA counts on\n180\tmulti-label LLP segments, split by the two pairwise diagnostic tags.\n181\t\n182\tWe propose SCoPE, a training-free framework for Sparse Cross-modal Prior Exchange. Stage 1\n183\tmakes all labels compete for the evidence in each time interval, reducing FCAs before cross-modal\n184\tguidance. Stage 2 uses events selected in one modality to lower the cost of selecting the same\n185\tevents in the other. The target modality then makes its own decision from its own features rather\n186\tthan copying the source score or prediction. A standard sparse readout cuts the sorted positive\n187\tcoefficients at their largest drop and therefore needs no calibrated cutoff. SCoPE requires no taskspecific training, instruction tuning, or LLM decoding.\n188\tOur contributions are:\n189\t• We define score-level false co-activation and show why one scalar cutoff on a fixed score\n190\tvector cannot reject an incorrect label without losing a correct one. We derive an exact\n191\tcondition for the two-label fit and test its prediction with the full 25-label dictionary.\n192\t• We introduce sparse cross-modal prior exchange. Events selected in one modality lower the\n193\tcost of selecting the same events in the other. Each modality still makes its own prediction\n194\tinstead of copying source scores or labels.\n195\t• Compared with the reported AV2 A values obtained with frozen CLIP+CLAP encoders,\n196\tSCoPE improves Type@seg by 7.45 points and Event@seg by 5.04 points on LLP.\n197\t• SCoPE’s sparse inference precedes final decision rules, so it can be combined with existing training-free post-processing. Adding AV2 A-style dynamic thresholds and candidate\n198\tfiltering raises Type@seg from 51.25 to 53.61 and Event@seg from 39.74 to 47.62 without\n199\tretraining. Inference takes 3.16 ms per video after feature encoding.\n200\t\n201\t2\n202\t\n203\tFALSE C O -ACTIVATION\n204\t\n205\tLet a video be divided into T aligned audio and visual segments, and let Y be the queried event\n206\tvocabulary. For modality m ∈ {a, v}, Ym (t) ⊆ Y is the ground-truth event set in segment t.\n207\tA frozen modality encoder produces zm (t), and the corresponding frozen text encoder maps the\n208\tcandidate names to\n209\tCm = [c1m , . . . , c|Y|\n210\tm ].\n211\tThe dictionary is encoded once and reused across videos. Dense training-free inference scores each\n212\tcandidate independently as\n213\tc\n214\tscos\n215\tm (t, c) = cos(zm (t), cm ).\n216\t\n217\tLet qm (t, c) denote the value that a method assigns to candidate c before making its final prediction.\n218\tFor example, after candidate filtering, qm (t, c) is the filtered score of each remaining candidate.\n219\t2\n220\t\n221\t\fDefinition 1 (False Co-Activation). Suppose a method retains at least one ground-truth label in a\n222\tsegment. A retained label j that is not in the ground truth is a false co-activation when\n223\tj∈\n224\t/ Ym (t),\n225\t\n226\tqm (t, j) ≥ min qm (t, i).\n227\ti∈Ym (t)\n228\ti retained\n229\t\n230\t(1)\n231\t\n232\tCandidate j is at or above the lowest-valued ground-truth label that remains. A scalar cutoff on the\n233\tsame values therefore cannot remove j while keeping every remaining ground-truth label. Definition 1 includes the more severe case in which j outranks every remaining ground-truth label.\n234\tFCA is a score-level diagnostic rather than a synonym for a final false positive. A class-specific\n235\tcutoff can keep the candidate out of the final prediction, but the FCA remains in the pre-readout\n236\tvalues.\n237\tIn a joint sparse fit, correlated labels no longer receive shared evidence separately. Once one label\n238\texplains their common direction, another is selected only if its remaining contribution justifies an\n239\tadditional selection cost. We next analyze the smallest sparse problem that contains this competition,\n240\twith one annotated event and one competing candidate.\n241\tLemma 1 (Two-Label Sparse Competition). Consider a centered unit embedding z (centering is\n242\tdefined in Section 3.2), a text atom ci for an annotated event, and a text atom cj for a candidate\n243\toutside the annotated set. Their two-label non-negative sparse fit is\n244\t2\n245\t\n246\tw∗ = arg min ∥z − [ci , cj ]w∥2 + λ0 ∥w∥1 .\n247\tw≥0\n248\t\n249\t(2)\n250\t\n251\t⊤\n252\t⊤\n253\tLet ρ = c⊤\n254\ti cj with 0 ≤ ρ < 1, and order the atoms so that ci z ≥ cj z. We restrict the analysis to\n255\tnon-negative correlation because the shadow/rival diagnostic concerns labels that share a positively\n256\taligned text direction. Negatively correlated atom pairs fall outside this diagnostic. Define γ = λ0 /2\n257\tand assume c⊤\n258\ti z > γ. The solution selects both atoms exactly when\n259\t\n260\t∆j|i (z) := (cj − ρci )⊤ z − γ(1 − ρ) > 0.\n261\t\n262\t(3)\n263\t\n264\tIf ∆j|i (z) ≤ 0, the fit selects only ci and removes candidate cj . Appendix A.1 gives the proof.\n265\tDefinition 2 (Shadow and Rival Co-Activations). Take an FCA j. We compare it with each\n266\trelated annotated event i that has at least as much dense evidence and can enter the sparse fit. These\n267\treference events form\n268\t\b\n269\t⊤\n270\t⊤\n271\t⊤\n272\tIj (z) = i ∈ Ym (t) : c⊤\n273\ti z ≥ cj z, ci z > γ, 0 < ci cj < 1 .\n274\tThe lemma permits ρ = 0, but the diagnostic below requires ρ > 0 because a shadow specifically\n275\tdenotes evidence shared by related atoms. For each i ∈ Ij (z), consider the two-label sparse problem\n276\tin Equation (2). We define\n277\tj is a shadow\n278\t\n279\t⇐⇒\n280\t\n281\t∃i ∈ Ij (z) : ∆j|i (z) ≤ 0,\n282\t\n283\tand call it a rival otherwise. In Figure 1(a), Dog remains selected while Cat disappears, so Cat is a\n284\tshadow. A rival is any FCA for which no eligible stronger annotated event removes the candidate\n285\tin this two-label analysis. This includes Ij (z) = ∅, where no such reference event exists. We\n286\treport this split only on multi-label segments to study a false candidate competing with several\n287\tsimultaneously annotated events rather than an ordinary single-label ranking error.\n288\tLemma 1 is exact only for two labels. With a full dictionary, several labels can jointly explain the\n289\tresidual and remove a rival FCA. Shadow and rival are therefore diagnostic tags, not hidden groundtruth causes or a one-to-one assignment to Stages 1 and 2. Appendix A.1 tests how the two-label\n290\tcondition relates to the full 25-label solve.\n291\t\n292\t3\n293\t\n294\tM ETHOD\n295\t\n296\t3.1\n297\t\n298\tP ROBLEM F ORMULATION\n299\t\n300\tGiven T aligned audio and visual segments and a queried event vocabulary Y, training-free AVEP\n301\tpredicts three binary decisions ŷa (t, c), ŷv (t, c), and ŷav (t, c) for every segment t and candidate c.\n302\tThe first two indicate event presence in the audio and visual branches, while ŷav denotes an event\n303\t3\n304\t\n305\t\fStage 0 · Dense Cosine\n306\t\n307\tStage 1 · Sparse Selection\n308\t\n309\tStage 2 · Re-Selection\n310\t\n311\tprediction heads\n312\tvisual\n313\tencoder\n314\t\n315\t❄\n316\t\n317\tz̃_v\n318\t\n319\tDog\n320\t\n321\tCat\n322\t\n323\tCar\n324\t\n325\tMotorcycle\n326\t\n327\tDog\n328\t\n329\tCat\n330\t\n331\tCar\n332\t\n333\tMotorcycle\n334\t\n335\tDog\n336\t\n337\tCat\n338\t\n339\tCar\n340\t\n341\tMotorcycle\n342\t\n343\t1\n344\t\n345\t1\n346\t\n347\t1\n348\t\n349\t1\n350\t\n351\t1.25↑\n352\t\n353\t1.25↑\n354\t\n355\t0.8↓\n356\t\n357\t0.7↓\n358\t\n359\tS_V = {Dog, Car}\n360\t\n361\tprior v→a\n362\tS_AV = {Car}\n363\t\n364\tprior a→v\n365\t\n366\taudio\n367\tencoder\n368\t\n369\t❄\n370\t\n371\tz̃_a\n372\t\n373\tDog\n374\t\n375\tCat\n376\t\n377\tCar\n378\t\n379\tMotorcycle\n380\t\n381\tcoefficient w per label\n382\t\n383\t1\n384\t\n385\t1\n386\t\n387\t1\n388\t\n389\t1\n390\t\n391\t1\n392\t\n393\t0.7↓\n394\t\n395\t1.35↑\n396\t\n397\t0.6↓\n398\t\n399\t1.35↑\n400\t\n401\tDog\n402\t\n403\tCat\n404\t\n405\tCar\n406\t\n407\tMotorcycle\n408\t\n409\tDog\n410\t\n411\tCat\n412\t\n413\tCar\n414\t\n415\tMotorcycle\n416\t\n417\tselection cost λ_c\n418\t\n419\t0.7↓\n420\t\n421\t0.7↓\n422\t\n423\tS_A = {Car}\n424\t\n425\tlowered by the prior from the other branch (audio → visual, visual → audio)\n426\t\n427\tFigure 2: SCoPE on the example from Figure 1. Stage 1 selects sparse support in each branch.\n428\tStage 2 lowers the selection costs of source-supported labels and re-solves from the target embedding. The AV head fuses coefficients retained by both branches.\n429\tthat is jointly audible and visible. The method receives the frozen segment embeddings zm (t) and\n430\ttext dictionaries Cm defined in Section 2, and uses no task-specific parameter training or segment\n431\tlabels.\n432\tSCoPE has two stages. Stage 1 fits the full event dictionary separately in each modality, making\n433\tthe candidates compete for the segment evidence. Stage 2 uses support from the other modality\n434\tto change candidate costs and then re-solves from the target embedding. Figure 2 gives the full\n435\tpipeline.\n436\t3.2\n437\t\n438\tC ENTERED E VENT D ICTIONARY\n439\t\n440\tMean subtraction [18] is a standard first step for removing common components from embedding\n441\tspaces. Contrastive multimodal encoders [14] also occupy modality-dependent offset regions. We\n442\ttherefore center and renormalize each modality before non-negative sparse inference:\n443\tz̃m (t) =\n444\t\n445\tzm (t) − µm\n446\t,\n447\t∥zm (t) − µm ∥2\n448\t\n449\tc̃cm =\n450\t\n451\tccm − µ̄m\n452\t.\n453\t∥ccm − µ̄m ∥2\n454\t\n455\t(4)\n456\t\n457\tHere µm is estimated from an external reference set and µ̄m is the mean of the queried text atoms.\n458\tWe write C̃m for the centered dictionary. Appendix B.1 specifies the references and Appendix C.2\n459\ttests sensitivity to the reference datasets.\n460\t3.3\n461\t\n462\tS TAGE 1: C OMPETITIVE S PARSE S ELECTION\n463\t\n464\tStage 1 makes all candidate labels compete to explain the same centered segment embedding. It\n465\tsolves non-negative Lasso independently in each modality:\n466\t2\n467\t\n468\twm (t) = arg min z̃m (t) − C̃m w\n469\tw≥0\n470\t\n471\t2\n472\t\n473\t+ λ0 ∥w∥1 .\n474\t\n475\t(5)\n476\t\n477\tEquation (5) differs from dense matching in how evidence is assigned. Cosine matching compares\n478\tevery text atom with the original embedding. The joint fit instead keeps an additional atom only\n479\twhen it reduces the remaining reconstruction error enough to justify another nonzero coefficient.\n480\tCorrelated labels therefore compete for shared evidence. This is the effect studied by the two-label\n481\tdiagnostic, but the diagnostic does not determine the outcome of the full Stage 1 solve.\n482\tThe selected support is\n483\tSm (t) = {c : wm (t, c) > εsupp }.\n484\tEquation (5) extends the two-atom fit in Equation (2) to all queried labels. Only this sparse,\n485\tcoefficient-weighted support is passed across modalities.\n486\t4\n487\t\n488\t\f3.4\n489\t\n490\tS TAGE 2: C ROSS -M ODAL C OST P RIOR AND TARGET R E -S ELECTION\n491\t\n492\tStage 2 uses support from the other modality to reconsider candidates that Stage 1 alone cannot\n493\tresolve. It exchanges a pooled soft prior, not dense scores or binary predictions. We describe visualto-audio guidance. The reverse direction uses the same construction with its own strength ηa→v .\n494\tFigure 1(b) illustrates the distinction. Visual support can lower the audio selection costs of Dog and\n495\tCar, but it does not add either visual score to the audio coefficients. Audio Stage 2 re-solves from\n496\tits own embedding, so Car can gain support without forcing the visually observed but silent Dog\n497\tinto the output.\n498\tReconstruction-weighted source prior. We measure how well the Stage 1 reconstruction fits each\n499\tsource segment and pool its normalized coefficients:\n500\tT\n501\th\n502\ti\n503\t1X\n504\twv (t, c)\n505\trv (t) = cos(z̃v (t), C̃v wv (t)) ,\n506\tPv (c) =\n507\trv (t)\n508\t.\n509\t(6)\n510\tT t=1\n511\t∥wv (t)∥∞ + εnorm\n512\t+\n513\tHere, [x]+ = max(0, x) and εnorm is a numerical stabilizer. We set rv (t) = 0 when wv (t) has\n514\tempty support. The prior retains class identity while allowing evidence at one time to guide another.\n515\tThe factor rv (t) givesP\n516\tless weight to poorly reconstructed source segments. We divide by the video\n517\tlength rather than by t rv (t), so consistently poor reconstruction also reduces the total guidance.\n518\tIf every rv (t) is zero, Stage 2 uses the Stage 1 cost. The infinity-norm normalization sets the largest\n519\tcoefficient in each nonempty segment to one, making source strength comparable across time while\n520\tpreserving the relative coefficient sizes within that segment.\n521\tFixed-budget event selection costs. The source prior first defines an unnormalized audio cost\n522\tmultiplier\n523\taa,c\n524\t.\n525\t(7)\n526\taa,c = exp(−ηv→a Pv (c)) ,\n527\tλa,c = λ0 1 P\n528\taa,j\n529\tP |Y| j\n530\tThe normalization keeps every cost positive and enforces c λa,c = |Y|λ0 . The prior therefore\n531\tredistributes a fixed budget rather than weakening sparsity as a whole. Stronger source support\n532\tlowers a class’s relative cost, while the other costs rise to preserve the mean. These costs are shared\n533\tacross the target video, but every segment is re-solved from its own embedding.\n534\tTarget re-selection. The audio branch then re-solves its own weighted NN-Lasso problem:\n535\tX\n536\t2\n537\tλa,c |wc |.\n538\twanew (t) = arg min z̃a (t) − C̃a w +\n539\tw≥0\n540\t\n541\t2\n542\t\n543\t(8)\n544\t\n545\tc\n546\t\n547\tThe reverse direction produces wvnew (t). A lower cost can help the target reconsider a weak label,\n548\tbut it cannot insert the source label or guarantee a correct selection. The unchanged reconstruction\n549\tterm still determines whether the target retains the candidate.\n550\t3.5\n551\t\n552\tS PARSE R EADOUT\n553\t\n554\tThe sparse coefficients can vary in absolute scale across segments, so a single calibrated cutoff is\n555\tnot appropriate for every output [15]. We instead use a classical one-dimensional gap split [11],\n556\tequivalent to cutting the longest edge of single-linkage clustering on the sorted coefficient values.\n557\tnew\n558\tnew\n559\tLet the positive Stage 2 coefficients be sorted as wm,(1)\n560\t(t) ≥ · · · ≥ wm,(K)\n561\t(t) > 0 and append the\n562\tnew\n563\tzero anchor wm,(K+1) (t) = 0. We select the largest gap:\n564\t\u0012\n565\th\n566\ti\u0013\n567\t∗\n568\tnew\n569\tnew\n570\t∗ (t)) }.\n571\tkm (t) = min arg max wm,(k) (t) − wm,(k+1) (t) ,\n572\tŶm (t) = {c(1) , . . . , c(km\n573\t(9)\n574\t1≤k≤K\n575\t\n576\tWe set ŷm (t, c) = I[c ∈ Ŷm (t)]. The outer minimum retains the smaller support when gaps tie.\n577\tEmpty support produces no prediction, and singleton support retains its label. The zero anchor lets\n578\tthe rule keep the full support when the final drop is strictly largest. The readout has no calibration\n579\tthreshold or support-size parameter.\n580\tnew\n581\tnew\n582\tFor audio-visual prediction, define Sm\n583\t(t) = {c : wm\n584\t(t, c) > εsupp }. We retain the support\n585\tintersection and fuse only its coefficients:\n586\tSav (t) = Sanew (t) ∩ Svnew (t),\n587\twav (t, c) = I[c ∈ Sav (t)] [αwanew (t, c) + (1 − α)wvnew (t, c)] .\n588\t(10)\n589\t\n590\t5\n591\t\n592\t\fApplying the same sparse readout to wav gives ŷav . For comparability, we inherit AV2 A’s\n593\tCLIP+CLAP fusion weight [20] and do not retune it by dataset. The audio and visual outputs\n594\tremain unchanged by this AV fusion. Appendix B.1 gives solver and temporal decoding details.\n595\t\n596\t4\n597\t\n598\tE XPERIMENTS\n599\t\n600\t4.1\n601\t\n602\tS ETUP\n603\t\n604\tDatasets and metrics. LLP [22] contains 25 event classes and modality-wise segment annotations\n605\tfor audio, visual, and audio-visual events. We report the standard segment-level and event-level F1\n606\tscores for Audio, Visual, Audio-Visual, Type, and Event. OV-AVEBench [28] contains 67 queried\n607\tclasses, for which we report chunk accuracy, segment F1, event F1, and their average. VGGSoundAVEL100k uses 141 VGGSound [3] event classes and evaluates temporal event localization using\n608\tsegment micro F1 and event F1. Appendix B.2 gives the split, temporal annotation, evaluated sample, and metric details.\n609\tFrozen encoders and dictionaries. The primary experiments use CLIP ViT-B/32 for vision and\n610\tLAION-CLAP HTSAT-tiny for audio. For CLIP+CLAP, we follow AV2 A’s released implementation. CLAP uses This is a sound of {label}, and CLIP uses A {label}. LanguageBind uses A {label} in both modalities. ImageBind uses the same modality-specific templates\n611\tas CLIP+CLAP. LLP uses 25 atoms, OV-AVEBench evaluates 67 event classes, and VGGSoundAVEL100k uses 141 atoms. Each dictionary is encoded once and reused for every video. Within\n612\teach table block, compared methods use the same frozen encoders, prompt templates, and vocabulary. We evaluate SCoPE with both CLIP+CLAP and LanguageBind on LLP, and with ImageBind\n613\ton OV-AVEBench and VGGSound-AVEL100k. The CLIP+CLAP OV-AVEBench block uses exactly the 67 evaluated event prompts. The ImageBind base and SCoPE rows share those prompts\n614\tplus an auxiliary other prompt, which is treated as background during evaluation.\n615\tReferences and implementation. We compute external feature means from MS-COCO 2017 for\n616\tvision and ESC-50 for audio. Section 3 specifies the inference settings. The base rows in Table 2 use\n617\tthe same frozen dense decoder across datasets. The ImageBind agreement row applies OV-AVEL’s\n618\tA/V agreement rule to the same dense scores. We quote the published LLP Dense and AV2 A rows\n619\tand reproduce the OV-AVEBench AV2 A row from its released predictions with the official evaluator.\n620\tAppendix B.1 gives the evaluation populations, solver, and preprocessing details.\n621\tHyperparameters. On the 563 official LLP validation videos with complete frozen feature caches,\n622\twe select the sparse cost and directional prior strengths. This gives λ0 = 0.30, ηv→a = 16, and\n623\tηa→v = 4. We use support tolerance εsupp = 10−6 and normalization stabilizer εnorm = 10−8 .\n624\tThe AV fusion weight α = 0.45 is inherited from AV2 A rather than selected on this subset. We\n625\tfreeze these values for LLP test evaluation and transfer them unchanged to the other backbones and\n626\tdatasets. Appendix B.2 reports the search grid, while Appendix C.1 reports sensitivity to the fixed\n627\tsettings.\n628\tRuntime protocol. We report cached-feature inference separately from the 393 ms encoding reference used by OV-AVEL. The AV2 A latency is the single-GPU equivalent of its reported 4×TITAN\n629\tRTX run. Appendix B.1 gives the measured scope and hardware.\n630\t4.2\n631\t\n632\tM AIN R ESULTS\n633\t\n634\tLLP. Table 1 compares training-free methods using the same frozen backbones and vocabularies\n635\twithin each block. With CLIP+CLAP, Stage 2 raises Audio, Visual, and Audio-Visual segment F1\n636\tover Stage 1 by 7.90, 8.15, and 11.70 points. Compared with the reported AV2 A values, it raises\n637\tType@seg from 43.8 to 51.25 and Event@seg from 34.7 to 39.74. With LanguageBind, the same\n638\tfixed configuration reaches 56.31 Type@seg and 46.01 Event@seg.\n639\tBenchmark and backbone transfer. Table 2 evaluates the same frozen SCoPE configuration on\n640\tlarger vocabularies and alternative backbones. On OV-AVEBench, SCoPE reaches 50.66 Avg. with\n641\tCLIP+CLAP and 53.90 with ImageBind, including 51.21 and 55.69 on unseen classes. On strict\n642\tVGGSound-AVEL100k, SCoPE reaches 49.63 segment F1 and 30.88 event F1 with CLIP+CLAP,\n643\tcompared with 28.47 and 24.21 for the AV2 A baseline. With ImageBind, OV-AVEL-style agreement\n644\t6\n645\t\n646\t\fTable 1: Training-free audio-visual video parsing on LLP (F1, %). LanguageBind follows AV2 A’s\n647\tadditional-backbone evaluation.\n648\tAudio\n649\t\n650\tMethod\n651\t\n652\tVisual\n653\t\n654\tAudio-Visual\n655\t\n656\tType\n657\t\n658\tEvent\n659\t\n660\tSeg\n661\t\n662\tEvt\n663\t\n664\tSeg\n665\t\n666\tEvt\n667\t\n668\tSeg\n669\t\n670\tEvt\n671\t\n672\tSeg\n673\t\n674\tEvt\n675\t\n676\tSeg\n677\t\n678\tEvt\n679\t\n680\tCLIP+CLAP [6, 19]\n681\twith AV2 A\n682\twith SCoPE (Stage 1)\n683\twith SCoPE (Stage 2)\n684\t\n685\t17.6\n686\t32.7\n687\t32.42\n688\t40.32\n689\t\n690\t15.1\n691\t28.5\n692\t23.98\n693\t31.47\n694\t\n695\t18.8\n696\t43.5\n697\t50.91\n698\t59.06\n699\t\n700\t17.8\n701\t41.5\n702\t42.29\n703\t52.39\n704\t\n705\t30.0\n706\t55.1\n707\t42.67\n708\t54.37\n709\t\n710\t25.6\n711